aps stay out of guilt that last  sentence casts this shadow of doubt  and indeed um claims that hewt was  racked with regret  and gave himself this heart-rending task  of beheading the giants  and making tombstones of sorts for them  with the swords that they once swung in  battle  this idea is reinforced by a little  gameplay twist where  if you resist the temptation to roll  around and destroy all the boxes in this  glorious display of particle physics  then if you don't do that huet will  speak with you  instead of fighting you we especially  adored the armor design here  which mirrors the armor of duran and i  loved his weapons of choice  a one perhaps for giants and one for men  this piece  marks a common trend in this competition  which is  the theme of humans committing really  evil deeds  and the whole race of giants going  extinct as a result  but one whose entry really broke the  mold  is icy with the widower  and the husks one of the cut content  speculations about the giants  was that they might have been a very  tight-knit communal civilization  since their archstone was granted to  them all rather than just  to one ruler icy honed in on this point  and created giants that were so bonded  together  that they actually take damage for each  other not only that but he interprets  the word  giants plural to mean that there are  only two  giants not necessarily a whole race as  we would expect  an abandoned journal in the wastes tells  of a man  exploring the frozen land after the fog  had swept through  he writes that he's looking for a stone  which we can assume to be the archstone  perhaps  and he goes past cities erased by fog  past beasts that hunt in the night  and he looks down over a ledge and spies  a masked black  demon so tall it scrapes the sky its  skin  is burnt from the snow the grooves on  its body are cracked  and it's surrounded by these weeping  husks it locks eyes with the man  who shrinks away and runs back to his  camp terrified  the next day in spite of himself he  returns to this place  and realizes that there are actually two  giants and one of them  is a corpse of a female lying on the  ground as he watches  the masked giant places its hand on its  partner's cheek  collapses to his knees and shrieks in  unison with all of the husks  filling the valley with sound to the  man's shock the female's chest  begins to rise and fall she was alive  and she's breathing  but looking down once more he sees the  husks turn as one  look at him and cry out brother come  down  you really have to read this one for  yourself the writing is incredibly  haunting it actually echoes lovecraft  all the way up  to the twist at the end furthermore the  gameplay also has some demon souls  gimmicks  you can fight the giant by slashing at  its heels or you can actually damage it  by hitting the bonded corpse before it  instead which is a little bit easier but  it will infuriate all of the husks as  well  if you read it you realize i see is the  god of narrative  and their full entry will stick with you  if you take the time to read it all  now all of these pieces are very high  concept  by which i mean they feature significant  bosses  big implications for the series and  really core concepts for the land of the  giants  but man not every entry can feature the  deepest law  sometimes we have to appreciate the  simpler things you know the levels that  lead up to these places  and that's where the great ravine by kyo  comes in the great ravine was once the  safest passage from boletaria  to the city of the giants that said it  was still dangerous  with dozens of conflicts between giants  and humans  fought to the taste of metal left upon  the tongue by a strange gas  that permeated the ravine but in one of  these conflicts something happened that  would change this place forever  a chance spark ignited the gas  triggering an explosion of flames along  the entire length of the valley  killing giants and humans alike and  co